nt controller–portfolio interaction across ten seeds (Appendix R ). We next fix the predictions to measure the effect of control directly. 4.2 Control makes a harmful candidate useful We use paired admission : deploy a challenger only when it makes fewer queried errors than the incumbent. Ties keep the incumbent. The historical PACS replay holds the incumbent, horizontal-view challenger and 816 batches fixed. Its Portal rule targets three symmetric-KL queries per batch; admission then equals ERM on those labels. Figure 3: Accuracy gains over the incumbent on the recorded PACS trajectory, including queried inputs. Portal uses symmetric-KL queries and paired ERM. Random gate matches its admission rate; random queries use three labels. Oracle uses full-batch labels. Appendix R.2 gives the full protocol. Table 1: Gain over the incumbent (pp). Selection uses paired admission on PACS/VLCS and sequential admission on ImageNet-C. Stream Always Selected VLCS -11.258 -0.353 PACS -5.797 +2.258 ImageNet-C +1.409 +0.590 Always deploying the challenger loses 5.80 points. Targeted feedback turns this into a 2.26-point gain (Figure 3 ). Random admission still loses accuracy, and uniform queries recover less of the gain. Excluding current queried inputs preserves the ordering: targeted queries gain 1.79 points and uniform queries gain 0.94. The useful intervention is unchanged; the evidence determines when to use it. VLCS tests a more harmful transfer. Always deploying its challenger loses 11.26 points; paired admission reduces the loss to 0.35. This avoids 96.9% of the fixed intervention’s net accuracy loss (Table 1 ). Control on a full corruption stream. In a shared-prefix variant, EATTA reuses the sequence of selection labels for its committed update. On 750,000 ImageNet-C images, error falls from 50.14% to 49.55%, improving all 15 corruptions. Of admitted batches, 77.3% benefit. Accepted choices stop after a mean prefix of 2.87 labels. Across all batches, the mean prefix is 3.99